ID: B2-Ch11b-Collars
TEXT:

 "Leila?..." Diane whispers back. "You mean the lessathi girl who was thrown in here with us two years ago? Listen, even if that caretaker is really trying to help us escape, you are still forgetting one thing."

Diane taps the band of cold metal locked around her throat—thick, dull seredium alloy, the same shock collar the golden fox clamped on us in the ruins.

"As long as Jason and I wear these collars, we don't make it past the perimeter," Diane says. "Step too far from the wards and they dump lightning into our spines. That's the end of our escape. But you don't have a collar. You're a reject. If you really trust this man, then you could--"

"No!" Kate catches herself before the shout carries down the corridor, dropping her voice to a harsh, urgent whisper. "You always do that! You always try to put your friends' needs before your own. I'm not going to let you do that now! We can all escape together. You, me, Leila, your brother--"

"I'm not sure how much Jason would agree with this plan," Diane says. "You know how he is. He never trusts anyone. Especially if they're lessathi. He was like this even before we knew about the test of loyalty. He barely even exchanges any words with the high-ranked lessathi that we are sent to escort on a regular basis. The only one he gets along with is that woman... Meridith. Their second in command. I don't know what he sees in her. He always tells me how she's different from the other high-ranked lessathi, but for me, they're all the same. They all know what's happening here. They're not blind. They're all to blame."

"Diane, listen to me!" Kate says. "That caretaker is very skilled at tinkering with magical devices. I'm sure that he could find a way to free you of your collars. I will talk to him about this with the first opportunity, but you need to find a way to convince your brother to come with us. Can you do that?"

"You're asking a lot of me, Kate..." Diane says. "If I tell Jason your story about how you came from the future, he'll think I'm insane. I'm not even sure how much I believe what you're saying. I mean... are you sure that it couldn't have been a hallucination of some sort? They've been feeding us really poorly, lately, and--"

"No!" Kate grips Diane's arm. "It's real! In a year from now, they're going to shut down the Beacon, due to lack of funds, and then they're going to throw all of us rejects in the jungle to fend for ourselves. I wasn't able to save even one of them... I can't let that happen again! I need to find a way to convince everyone. Just promise me that you'll try to talk to your brother. You don't need to tell him that I'm from the future. Just try to find a way to get through to him. Somehow..."

"I'll... try..." Diane says, looking down at the stone floor.

"Thank you," Kate says. "I will come to talk to you again, after speaking with the caretaker about the collars. I'm sure we'll be able to work something out. See you soon!"

Kate slips out of the cell. In the empty air beside me, Arraka's voice rattles inside my head. "Right. I took a quick look through the following events, and I think it should be safe to skip the next part. I'll just go ahead and--"

"Wait," I say. "What's the next part about?"

"Oh," Arraka says, "it's just some uninteresting stuff about her talking with the caretaker, and him confirming that it's possible to take off the collars. It's a waste of time."

choice(""But I want to see it!"", B2-Ch11b-Skip1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Skip1, v_b2_ch11_skip1 = 0)
choice(""I suppose it would be better to skip it, then."", B2-Ch11b-Skip1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Skip1, v_b2_ch11_skip1 = 1)
choice(""Could you show me Diane's discussion with her brother, instead, then?"", B2-Ch11b-Skip1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Skip1, v_b2_ch11_skip1 = 2)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Skip1
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_skip1 == 0) {
 "But I want to see it!"

"Fine, whatever..." Arraka mutters. "Don't say I didn't warn you, though."

The corridor dissolves and snaps into another cramped holding cell—Kate's room. Kate stands alone near the barred window, speaking in low tones with someone hidden outside against the stone wall. I can't see his face, but the grease-stained cuffs and quiet, measured voice match the lessathi Leila calls her father.

"Those collars will be tricky to deal with," the caretaker says through the bars. "With the right tools, I can dismantle the locking runes. It will take time, however, and it cannot be done on the run. What I can do instead is disable the lethal discharge until we are safely clear of the perimeter. It leaves your magic suppressed, but the wards will not kill you the moment you step outside the Beacon."

"Yes, that is exactly what we need!" Kate says. "Thank you! There was also something else I wanted to talk to you about. You should stop telling the other orphans about your escape plan. We've been tricked before, by someone who made a similar offer, and many are now reluctant to trust any of the caretakers because of that. If you're not careful, someone is going to report you to the higher ups, and the escape plan will be ruined. I have a plan on how to convince them to trust you, but you will need to give me some time. Come back tomorrow, and we'll talk more about this."

"Very well," the lessathi says softly. "I must return to my duties before the patrol notices my absence. We will speak tomorrow."

Footsteps recede down the gravel path outside.

"Finally!" Arraka drawls. "Riveting conversation. Now let's move on to the events that actually matter."

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_skip1 == 1) {
 "I suppose it would be better to skip it, then."

"A fine choice," Arraka says. "Let's move."

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_skip1 == 2) {
 "Could you show me Diane's discussion with her brother, instead, then?"

"I suppose..." Arraka says. "I doubt it changes the outcome, but at least it won't be a complete waste of our time."

The room remains the same, but the light shifts. Diane stands near the rough wooden bunk, talking with a blond teenager in threadbare rags. Jason. He isn't broad or particularly tall, but his posture carries a hard, calculated edge that doesn't belong to a boy his age—shoulders square, chin up, cold focus in his eyes. A shock collar encircles his throat, matching his sister's.

"Diane," Jason says, folding his arms. "I've told you many times before that you should take everything Kate tells you with a grain of salt. I know that she is your best friend, but that girl is way too trusting for her own good. When you put so much trust into people, it hurts that much more when you inevitably get betrayed. Mark my words, one day, she will get betrayed so badly, that she will never want to trust anyone ever again, and when that happens, you won't be able to tell me that I didn't warn you..."

"So, you're saying, what... that she should be more like you?" Diane says. "Ever since I've known you, you've never trusted anyone other than yourself. How is that any better?"

"Hey, don't say things like that..." Jason says, his voice dropping. "You know that I've always trusted you. Even if the whole world betrays us, you and I will still continue to stay true to each other, until the very end. That's the promise we made, right before we were first assigned as elite bodyguards to the lessathi, three years ago. You still remember that promise, right?"

"Of course I do!" Diane says. "But do YOU still remember it? I've been telling you for years that Meridith is bad news, and that you should keep your distance from her, but you never listen! Why is it that you are so willing to put your trust in her, when you've never trusted anyone in your life?"

"I never said I trust her!" Jason says. "She's just... a means to an end. That's all there is to it. And besides, she's not like the other lessathi. She has the potential to change things for the better. All she needs is a little push in the right direction. There are already many lessathi who support her. I'd even go as far as saying that there are more who currently support her, than the ones who support the great leader, Heksol. It's not by coincidence, that she managed to become second in command. When she first started off, she was only a lowly secretary to Heksol, but she's been climbing her way up the ranks for twenty years, and she's made many friends during that time. I'm telling you, there will be a great change coming soon, in the lessathi world, and I want to be right at the heart of it, when it happens!"

"You know I don't like it when you talk like this..." Diane says, shaking her head. "I thought that our goal was to escape this place. Why would we care about the lessathi world, and how they change for the better? Let them rot, for all I care! I only want to be free of this nightmare, so we can live the rest of our lives in peace together. All three of us."

"And we will!" Jason says. "What I'm doing is for the sake of all of us. Kate included. You'll understand when you're older."

"You always say this!" Diane says. "What about Kate's plan, regarding the lessathi caretaker? Are you at least going to consider it?"

"I'll... think about it," Jason says, glancing toward the door. "Anyway, I need to get going. If I stay too long, the guards will suspect something. Take care!"

"Well, that was moderately illuminating," Arraka says. "Now let's get back to the stuff that's actually relevant to this ritual."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Olivia, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Olivia)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Olivia
TEXT:

The air folds, and we materialize in another stone cell where Kate is speaking with a girl of about fifteen with light brown hair. She wears the same tattered gray rags as Kate and Diane, but her hair is combed neat, and her posture is composed and deliberate. She carries an easy smile and a warm, charismatic cadence that instantly draws people in.

"Please," Kate says. "You are the only one who can help me. The other orphans never listen to me, but I'm sure they'll listen to you! You've always had a way with words, and everyone trusts your judgment. You're almost like a leader, of sorts."

"You flatter me, Kate!" the girl says, her smile tightening. "But this isn't an easy task you are asking of me. Do you not remember what happened the last time when we decided to trust one of the caretakers? The lessathi chose to kill only one of us, back then, in order to set an example, but that could have been any one of us. They had no reason to choose Benjamin, out of all of us. He didn't stand out in any way. He didn't even say a word. But they simply kept beating him and beating him in front of us, and they just wouldn't stop. Even after he was dead, they still wouldn't stop. If we try to escape again, and they catch us, I don't think that they will settle for killing only one person, anymore. This time, they're going to kill all of us, and they're not going to show us any mercy..."

"They're going to try to kill us anyway!" Kate says. "We're both rejects. When they'll finally be done experimenting on us, they'll throw us in a jungle, and they'll leave us for dead."

"We can't stay rejects forever!" the girl says, though her eyes dart away. "Maybe one day we'll be like your friend, Diane, and her brother, and we'll get to escort important lessathi around instead of being experimented on, over and over. If we haven't died from the experiments for such a long time, then there has to still be a chance!"

"It's not going to happen, Olivia," Kate says. "We can't just keep waiting for things to get better. We need to take matters into our own hands. Please, trust me on this! Following the caretaker's plan is our only true chance of escaping this hellhole. He's not going to betray us like the one before. We can still make it out of here alive, but I need your help!"

Olivia falls silent, the uncertainty on her face hardening into steady resolve.

"I understand, Kate," Olivia says softly. "You know that I've always been your friend. If you are willing to put so much trust into this man, then I will believe in you, and your judgment. I will try to convince as many people as I can, but I can't promise you anything. I'll tell you later how things went."

"Thank you so much!" Kate says, relief easing the tension in her face. "I will also talk to Diane and tell her what you said. We'll talk more when you have something new to tell me. See you later!"

"Goodbye!" Olivia says, offering that pleasant smile again as Kate turns and exits the room.

"Okay, this next part's going to be really good," Arraka cackles. "Get ready!"

The stone walls stretch and dissolve, dropping us into a cavernous underground hallway. Around a hundred orphans stand in rigid, silent ranks, facing several men in dark blue robes near the platform. One lessathi is down on his knees in the center of the flagstones, a heavy hood hiding his bowed head.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Malnourished, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Malnourished)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Malnourished
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_skip1 == 2) {
The orphans stand in identical ash-gray rags. Most look hollowed out, dark rings carved deep beneath their eyes, bodies thin to the bone and mottled with yellowing bruises. Ages range from teenagers down to four-year-olds clutching older hands, but most fall between six and fifteen. The only two separated from the ranks are Diane and her brother, standing beside the robed lessathi in their bodyguard positions.

The lessathi murmur among themselves while the children wait in dead, frightened silence. A couple more officials are still striding down the far end of the tunnel before the assembly begins.

"Arraka," I mutter after several seconds of silence. "You couldn't drop us closer to the actual announcement? How long are we supposed to stand here?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_skip1 != 2) {
The orphans stand in identical ash-gray rags. Most look hollowed out, dark rings carved deep beneath their eyes, bodies thin to the bone and mottled with yellowing bruises. Ages range from teenagers down to four-year-olds clutching older hands, but most fall between six and fifteen. The only two separated from the ranks are Diane and a blond teenager standing beside the robed lessathi in bodyguard positions.

The boy isn't broad, but his posture carries a sharp, calculating maturity far beyond his years. A heavy metal collar circles his neck, matching Diane's.

The lessathi murmur among themselves while the children wait in dead, frightened silence. A couple more officials are still striding down the far end of the tunnel before the assembly begins.

"Arraka," I mutter after several seconds of silence. "You couldn't drop us closer to the actual announcement? How long are we supposed to stand here?"

}
 "Stop complaining," Arraka snaps. "I brought us here early because I have a hypothesis to test. I've had a theory for a while about why the lessathi excel at crafting magical devices. The short version is that their minds possess an innate grasp of the Magium's underlying structure, allowing them to tune an artifact's aura far more precisely than any mortal enchanter. But it goes beyond theoretical knowledge. Fyron knows the Magium's nature and has a channel to it, yet that staff of his is clumsy compared to lessathi tech. It's as though they developed a natural affinity over centuries—subtle enough that ordinary aura scans miss it entirely. If that holds true, it also explains why lessathi auras carry no ambient magic. If their subconscious resonates constantly with the Magium, the sheer volume of raw data flooding their minds would completely jam any connection to the magical plane."

"Right now you're the one flooding my mind," I say. "What do you mean their auras resonate with the Magium? I'm half-lessathi. Are you claiming I've had the Magium humming in my skull this whole time? Because I certainly haven't felt it."

"Of course not," Arraka sneers. "Did the tournament organizers promise you affinity, or did they promise access? Having a dull resonance is useless if you have no way to process the incoming flow, especially since your human blood waters it down. But grant someone direct access to the Magium on the earthen plane, and that latent affinity turns into an immense advantage. The lessathi on Varathia already hold direct access. That's why their craft works. But like I said—merely a theory until I run a test."

choice(""What kind of test are you talking about?"", B2-Ch11b-Test, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Test)
choice(""Why would the lessathi have direct access to the Magium?"", B2-Ch11b-Direct, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Direct)
choice(""How do you think the lessathi might have gotten subconsciously linked to the Magium?"", B2-Ch11b-Subconsciously, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Subconsciously)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Test
TEXT:

 "What kind of test are you talking about?"

"I'm checking whether any of the lessathi across the hall can sense our presence," Arraka says. "I've been broadcasting along different magical wavelengths for minutes, but nothing has registered. If my theory holds, our active link to the Magium right now should make us detectable to them, even though we aren't physically anchored in their timeline. Perhaps subtle frequencies are too quiet. Let's try something louder."

Three painted clowns materialize in the empty space between the lessathi and the orphans. Grinning wide, they begin to stomp and beat their chests in exaggerated ape pantomime.

"Much better," Arraka says. "Though it still lacks flair. Hey, Flower, why don't you join your colleagues? You're already wearing the outfit."

"I'm not wearing clown clothes!" Flower snaps, sparks flaring around her hands. "How many times do I have to say it? These are acrobat clothes! Trapeze attire from Olmnar!"

"Yes, yes, fascinating," Arraka says. "Now go dance with the troupe so we can--"

"Did you feel that?" one of the lessathi guards mutters to the man next to him.

"Feel what?" the other replies.

"A pulse... right in front of us," the first says, narrowing his eyes at the empty air where the illusions dance. "Like a presence that doesn't belong to this world. As if it exists and doesn't exist at the same time. You didn't catch it?"

"If it doesn't exist, stop wasting my time with it," the second lessathi grunts. "Stand straight and wait for the overseer like the rest of us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Warning, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Warning)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Direct
TEXT:

 "Why would the lessathi have direct access to the Magium?"

"They're allied with the kings of Varathia," Arraka says. "They built the foundation for this tournament. If the crowns hold access to the Magium, the lessathi share the tap."

"Right," I say. "So what kind of test are you planning to do, exactly?"

"I'm checking whether any of the lessathi across the hall can sense our presence," Arraka says. "I've been broadcasting along different magical wavelengths for minutes, but nothing has registered. If my theory holds, our active link to the Magium right now should make us detectable to them, even though we aren't physically anchored in their timeline. Perhaps subtle frequencies are too quiet. Let's try something louder."

Three painted clowns materialize in the empty space between the lessathi and the orphans. Grinning wide, they begin to stomp and beat their chests in exaggerated ape pantomime.

"Much better," Arraka says. "Though it still lacks flair. Hey, Flower, why don't you join your colleagues? You're already wearing the outfit."

"I'm not wearing clown clothes!" Flower snaps, sparks flaring around her hands. "How many times do I have to say it? These are acrobat clothes! Trapeze attire from Olmnar!"

"Yes, yes, fascinating," Arraka says. "Now go dance with the troupe so we can--"

"Did you feel that?" one of the lessathi guards mutters to the man next to him.

"Feel what?" the other replies.

"A pulse... right in front of us," the first says, narrowing his eyes at the empty air where the illusions dance. "Like a presence that doesn't belong to this world. As if it exists and doesn't exist at the same time. You didn't catch it?"

"If it doesn't exist, stop wasting my time with it," the second lessathi grunts. "Stand straight and wait for the overseer like the rest of us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Warning, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Warning)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Subconsciously
TEXT:

 "How do you think the lessathi might have gotten subconsciously linked to the Magium?"

"No idea," Arraka says. "Ancient lessathi texts mention an obsession with the Magium spanning millennia, so perhaps constant exposure shaped them over generations. But if you want the exact mechanism, your guess is as good as mine."

"Fine," I say. "So what kind of test were you planning to do, exactly?"

"I'm checking whether any of the lessathi across the hall can sense our presence," Arraka says. "I've been broadcasting along different magical wavelengths for minutes, but nothing has registered. If my theory holds, our active link to the Magium right now should make us detectable to them, even though we aren't physically anchored in their timeline. Perhaps subtle frequencies are too quiet. Let's try something louder."

Three painted clowns materialize in the empty space between the lessathi and the orphans. Grinning wide, they begin to stomp and beat their chests in exaggerated ape pantomime.

"Much better," Arraka says. "Though it still lacks flair. Hey, Flower, why don't you join your colleagues? You're already wearing the outfit."

"I'm not wearing clown clothes!" Flower snaps, sparks flaring around her hands. "How many times do I have to say it? These are acrobat clothes! Trapeze attire from Olmnar!"

"Yes, yes, fascinating," Arraka says. "Now go dance with the troupe so we can--"

"Did you feel that?" one of the lessathi guards mutters to the man next to him.

"Feel what?" the other replies.

"A pulse... right in front of us," the first says, narrowing his eyes at the empty air where the illusions dance. "Like a presence that doesn't belong to this world. As if it exists and doesn't exist at the same time. You didn't catch it?"

"If it doesn't exist, stop wasting my time with it," the second lessathi grunts. "Stand straight and wait for the overseer like the rest of us."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Warning, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Warning)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Warning
TEXT:

 "Aha!" Arraka banishes the clowns with a snap. "I knew it! The resonance is real. Though if the lessathi hold such a natural affinity, how did Eiden and the golden fox figure out how to conjure food from nothing before they did? Wasn't that knowledge pulled from the Magium too? Hmm..."

"You do realize," I say, "if you keep handing me pieces of the puzzle, I'm going to work out what the Magium actually is."

"And why would I care?" Arraka scoffs. "It's all entertainment. I only withhold it because your frustration makes me laugh. I never took an oath of silence from the Magium like Flower and Illuna."

I freeze. "What did you just say?..."

"Arraka, stop," Illuna snaps, blue light sparking behind her irises, a hard edge of dread cutting through her voice. "Not another word. I'm warning you!"

"Warning me?" Arraka's voice splits into three mocking registers. "Warning ME? Has this spectator trick scrambled your senses? You're speaking to Arraka! Gods tremble at my name! You seriously think I fear the Magium? It's a tool! A mechanism I can twist around my fingers without breaking a sweat! Why would I share your petty mortal terror? Today's your lucky day, Barry! You want to know what the Magium is? Piece together what you've seen, weigh it against what you already know, and the truth staring you right in the face is that the Magium is in fact--"

The sentence never finishes.

A sensation hits me—not pain, not pressure, but something so absolute that every threat I've ever faced from Arraka or Selkram evaporates into triviality. This isn't fear of death. It is the raw horror of non-existence.

The world stutters. Color leaches out of the stone in a heartbeat, then the hall, the children, the lessathi, and the ground dissolve entirely. There is no dark, no light, no space—only a hollow void extending endlessly across nothing.

In that fraction of a second, stretching out into an agonizing eternity, the concept of me ceases to exist. Not killed. Erased. Plucked clean from the fabric of reality as if I had never been thought of, never been born, never taken a single breath. And yet some fragment of consciousness remains stranded in the blankness, screaming into an absolute vacuum.

Color slams back into place.

I find myself in my semi-transparent form again in the underground corridor. Cold sweat drenches my skin, and my pulse hammers in my ears like a war drum. Beside me, Flower stands motionless, staring blankly ahead, trembling so hard her teeth click.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Arrogant, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Arrogant)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Arrogant
TEXT:

 "You arrogant fool!!!" Illuna's voice tears through the silence as she snatches the amulet and thrusts it before her face, her eyes blazing brilliant cerulean. "Do you realize what you've done?!"

I haven't seen Illuna this furious since the ogre fort when Arraka recited the sacred woods massacre. Arraka offers no quip, no insult. The stone walls stutter again. The corridor dissolves and reassembles in rapid spasms—gray, void, gray, void—tearing my grip on reality loose before slamming me back onto the floor, gasping for air.

"Are you going to just stay silent until we get erased from existence?!" Illuna yells into the gold.

"Shut up!" A raw tremor runs through Arraka's voice. "I'm weaving a diversion! I can't anchor the thread if you keep screaming in my ear. Just... give me a minute!"

The world drains of color again, the rapid flicker intensifying until the void threatens to swallow my mind permanently. My fingers claw at empty space. Then, with a violent jolt, the tether snaps taut and reality locks solid. The gray corridor returns. Flower and I are both panting, our chests heaving in ragged sync.

"Right..." Arraka mutters, the rattle still lingering in her throat. "That should hold. The resonance is masked."

"What do you mean that should hold?" Illuna demands. "Did you solve the problem or not?"

"Yes! I think. I don't know!" Arraka snaps. "I've never had to evade the core itself! I diverted the Magium's attention using some tricks I lifted from the God of Fate. He pulled a similar stunt when they tampered with the Magium to cast me out of the magical plane. If the gods slipped away intact, we should be clear."

"You cast an illusion?" I ask, wiping cold sweat from my brow.

"More like an absurdly layered suggestion weave," Arraka says. "Its gaze is scouring another timeline entirely right now. As long as I manage to keep my idiot mouth shut this time around, it shouldn't trace the echo back here."

"So you admit that you were an idiot, then?" Illuna says, flat and merciless.

"Yes!" Arraka shrieks. "Yes, I admit it! Are you happy now? I was an idiot to try and challenge the Magium in its own territory. All of my thousands of years of knowledge are nearly useless, when faced with this world of contradictions and absurdities. Now let's calm ourselves down, and finish doing what we originally came here to do. I no longer want to spend any more time in here than is absolutely necessary."

choice(""Then how about bringing us to the actual start of the lessathi meeting?"", B2-Ch11b-Individual, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Individual, v_b2_ch11_skip2 = 0)
choice(""Can we just skip directly to the important parts, then?"", B2-Ch11b-Individual, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Individual, v_b2_ch11_skip2 = 1)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Individual
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_skip2 == 0) {
 "Then how about bringing us to the actual start of the lessathi meeting?" I say. "We've been wasting enough time here as it is."

"Fine, fine," Arraka mutters.

Time blurs forward, shadows shifting along the walls until the last of the lessathi march into the corridor to join their colleagues. Once everyone is assembled, the meeting begins.

"You may all be wondering why we've called you here," begins a gray-haired lessathi with a trimmed beard, appearing in his late fifties. "I will keep this brief. It has come to our attention that a few among you have been approached by a certain individual, offering to assist in an escape from our facility. As you know, regulations obligate you to report such solicitations immediately. Not only was that duty neglected, but several subjects agreed to participate. A grave disappointment. This assembly serves two purposes."

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_skip2 == 1) {
 "Can we just skip directly to the important parts, then?"

"This IS one of the important parts," Arraka says. "Though there's a later exchange between Kate and her friend we can trim. Let me skip straight to the announcement—my patience is running thin."

Time blurs forward, shadows shifting along the walls until the last of the lessathi march into the corridor to join their colleagues. Once everyone is assembled, the meeting begins.

"You may all be wondering why we've called you here," begins a gray-haired lessathi with a trimmed beard, appearing in his late fifties. "I will keep this brief. It has come to our attention that a few among you have been approached by a certain individual, offering to assist in an escape from our facility. As you know, regulations obligate you to report such solicitations immediately. Not only was that duty neglected, but several subjects agreed to participate. A grave disappointment. This assembly serves two purposes."

}
 The speaker steps behind the hooded lessathi kneeling on the flagstones.

"The first reason is to execute the traitor who conspired against this facility," the bearded lessathi says.

He grips the kneeling man's head with both hands and twists sharply. A sickening snap echoes down the hall; the caretaker slumps forward, dead before he hits the stone. Kate stifles a sharp gasp, slamming a hand over her mouth. Near the center of the orphan ranks, eight-year-old Leila, silver hair hanging over her shoulders, stares at the corpse, frozen in terror.

"The second reason," the bearded lessathi continues, stepping past the body, "is to offer one final opportunity for those who accepted his proposition. You may step forward and confess. Those who present themselves voluntarily will receive mitigated re-education. If you attempt to hide, however, the penalty will be far worse."

Silence stretches across the corridor. Then a six-year-old boy and a ten-year-old girl step out of line, trembling as they approach the platform. The boy is already fighting back tears.

"A sensible decision," the bearded lessathi says, resting a hand on each of their shoulders. "Acknowledging error is an important step forward toward your re-education. However, you are not the only ones who were ready to leave with the traitor. I am still waiting for the rest to turn themselves in. It would not be in your best interest to keep me waiting much longer..."

Kate's entire body shakes. She crosses her arms over her chest, digging her nails into her sleeves in a futile effort to steady herself.

After ten seconds of dead silence, the lessathi shakes his head in disapproval.

"I was hoping it would not come to this..." the lessathi says. "Very well. If you insist on disobeying us even now, then I have no choice but to call your names myself. First, I would like to introduce the person who played the most crucial role in allowing us to identify every culprit. Olivia, step forward."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Secretly, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Secretly)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Secretly
TEXT:

 "Yes, sir," Olivia says in a quiet, timid voice, stepping from the line to stand beside the lessathi, turning to face the other orphans.

Across the hall, Kate goes completely rigid. The color drains from her face, and the tremor in her hands turns violent.

"Olivia has secretly served as our informant for quite a while," the bearded lessathi says. "Her earlier contributions were modest, but today she demonstrated exceptional utility. She came to us to warn us of the traitor, and warned us that others intended to join him. She then agreed to carry an active transceiver, allowing us to monitor her conversations with the traitor and every child who agreed to flee. With our documentation complete, denial is futile. Step forward as your name is called."

He clears his throat loudly before continuing.

"There are four names on our list. William, Hannah, Lillian, and Matthew. Do not waste our time by pretending you did not hear. We all know what happens to those who openly disobey."

The four orphans step forward, eyes fixed on the stone floor, their limbs shaking in silence.

"Good," the lessathi says. "Starting tomorrow, you report to room fifty-one to commence your re-education process. Is that clear?"

"No, please, not fifty-one!" a thirteen-year-old boy cries, tears spilling over his cheeks. "Anything but that! Have mercy, I beg you!"

"Do not make the mistake of assuming you have any choice in the matter," the bearded lessathi says coldly. "Every complaint you utter from this moment on adds more time to the penalty for all four of you. Now tell me, do you have any more complaints?"

"No, sir..." the boy whispers, choking on a sob.

"Splendid," the lessathi says. "Now, Olivia, one final question. Were there any other orphans who sought to conspire with the traitor? Perhaps someone you spoke with before collecting the transceiver?"

Olivia's eyes flick toward Kate for a fraction of a second, then lock straight ahead again.

"No, sir," Olivia says. "There was no one else."

"Very well," the lessathi says. "You will be rewarded for your efforts. You may be a reject, but even for someone like you, there is a place in our organization, provided you remain useful."

"Yes, sir," Olivia murmurs, bowing her head. "That is all I've ever wanted. To be useful..."

"Take these conspirators to a temporary holding cell until arrangements are complete," the lessathi instructs his colleagues. "Tomorrow, we begin their re-education."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Treatment, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Treatment)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Treatment
TEXT:

 "No!" Kate screams, stepping clear of the ranks. "You can't take them! They did nothing wrong!"

The trembling in her limbs vanishes, replaced by raw, furious defiance. Every single person in the hall turns to stare at her. The bearded lessathi walks toward her at a slow, deliberate pace.

"What did you say?..." he asks quietly once he reaches her.

"I said they did nothing wrong!" Kate's voice wavers slightly, but she holds her ground. "All they wanted was to escape this hellhole of a place. How can you blame them for wanting that?"

"Strange..." the lessathi murmurs, studying her. "I don't remember you ever being brave enough to stand up to us before. You were always timid and obedient. Your aura reads differently as well. I wonder what caused such a drastic shift in your personality... Perhaps we should dissect you and find out. You are a reject, after all. Your loss will hardly disrupt our operations."

"Leave her alone!" Diane shouts from the far side of the platform, tears shining in her eyes. "Don't you dare touch her!"

"What is this?" The bearded lessathi turns toward Diane, his tone sharpening. "A rebellion among the guard caste? Do you imagine you receive special treatment just because you are one of our elite bodyguards? Or did you forget that you cannot cast magic with that collar around your neck unless we specifically authorize it?"

He closes the distance and grabs Diane by the hair, wrenching her head up so she is forced to look directly into his eyes.

"Do you think you are beyond our reach because you escort our leaders? Do you think you no longer face punishment? Perhaps room fifty-one would suit you as well. I'm sure the leadership would not mind a more obedient servant..."

"What is the meaning of this?!" a sharp female voice echoes down the corridor.

A lessathi woman in dark blue robes strides into the hall, flanked by two armed guards. She appears in her late thirties, with long brown hair, green eyes, and an unmistakable aura of authority.

"Lady Meridith..." The bearded lessathi releases Diane immediately, stepping back. "I thought that you and Lord Heksol had already finished your inspection of the Beacon..."

"Lord Heksol departed early this morning," Meridith says coldly. "I remained to settle administrative matters. What were you doing just now to my personal bodyguard?"

"Lady Meridith!" Diane says in a shaky voice. "They were trying to kill Kate! They said they wanted to dissect her for speaking out!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Development, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Development)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Development
TEXT:

 "Kate?..." Meridith looks toward the eleven-year-old girl. "The friend you mentioned?"

"Allow me to explain, Lady Meridith!" the bearded lessathi intervenes, glaring at Kate with venom. "This reject attempted to interfere with our disciplinary procedures!"

"Disciplinary procedures?" Meridith asks.

"Precisely!" the lessathi says. "Those four orphans standing over there have broken our rules and must be re-educated so they no longer cause problems. But this reject attempted to give us orders, claiming we had no right to take them away!"

"What exactly is this re-education process you keep mentioning?" Meridith says, her tone dropping several degrees.

"The re-education process is an extreme measure we employ against habitual rule-breakers," the lessathi explains smoothly. "Subjects are taken to isolation rooms and subjected to continuous conditioning over several months until they become completely obedient. The techniques dismantle every ounce of free will while preserving their intellect, allowing them to comprehend and execute our orders flawlessly. Subjects emerge completely docile, ceasing peer interaction and speaking only when addressed. In short, ideal test subjects. We have refined the methodology over years and take great pride in our recent results."

"You dare say all this to my face?" Meridith's eyes flash with cold fury. "And you present these barbarities as an accomplishment deserving of praise? What in the gods' names is wrong with you? These are children! When I reviewed the experimental protocols here, I had severe misgivings, but I never imagined you would go this far!"

"Lady Meridith..." the bearded lessathi falters. "Lord Heksol already approved--"

"I do not care what Lord Heksol approved!" Meridith cuts him off. "Bring me your personnel ledger. Bring me a complete list of your procedures and conditioning manuals. Now. If I find one more insane technique documented, I will strike it from the record—and if you ever employ them again, you will answer with your life. Do you understand me?"

"Yes, madam..." the lessathi says, bowing hastily. "I will retrieve the documents at once."

"And the rest of you!" Meridith turns on the other lessathi staff. "I will hear your accounts right now. Omit nothing, unless you wish to anger me further."

"Well, look at that," Arraka muses. "Meridith, the lessathi's current grand leader. I wonder how their management shifted in the years after she took over..."

choice(""I'd say not much, given what we've heard from Golmyck."", B2-Ch11b-Golmyck, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Golmyck)
choice(""What I'm wondering is if she had a hand in the shutting down of the Beacon."", B2-Ch11b-Shutting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Shutting)
choice(""Well, Diane is no longer wearing a collar, so some things have definitely changed."", B2-Ch11b-Collar, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Collar)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Golmyck
TEXT:

 "I'd say not much, given what we've heard from Golmyck."

"Why?" Arraka says. "Because they encourage slavery? Slavery is treated as an ordinary part of society across Varathia. When I speak of management, I mean the practices that actually need to be hidden from the public, like the torture and brainwashing happening at this 'Beacon of Hope'."

"Well, they never built a second Beacon, so there's that," I say.

"Arraka," Illuna interrupts, "shouldn't we be focusing on escaping this place instead of having casual conversations?"

"I AM focusing on escaping," Arraka says. "The only reason I'm continuing with the ritual is to keep my nerves steady while I map an escape plan."

"Why can't we just leave the way we entered?" Illuna says.

"Because I'm not sure if the boundary is safe!" Arraka snaps. "Normally, I would waltz right out of here without a second thought, but carelessness is what got us into this mess in the first place. Now I'm verifying every seam. As long as we stay within the ritual frame, we're masked, but if we breach the perimeter carelessly, we may alert the Magium by mistake."

"You think the exits are compromised?" Illuna asks.

"I don't know!" Arraka says. "I assumed the Magium remained in a state of constant hibernation unless someone tampered with primordial laws, like when Memphir banished me from the magical plane. I didn't think it would stir simply from me revealing its nature! And now that it's alert, I don't know how wide its perception reaches. We are going to keep spectating rituals until I am absolutely certain the path out is clear."

"Fine..." Illuna mutters. "Do what you think is best."

Across the hall, Meridith finishes interrogating the lessathi staff and dismisses them.

"When you see the caretaker bringing the documents, send him to my temporary office," Meridith instructs the departing guards.

She turns toward her personal escort.

"Go on ahead. I'll join you in a moment."

As her bodyguards step into the corridor, Meridith approaches Kate, leaning down to speak in a low voice into her ear.

"What you did back there was very brave," Meridith says. "But bravery by itself is worthless unless you also have the power to enforce your own ideals upon others. Remember this advice."

She taps Kate lightly on the shoulder and strides out after her guards.

A brilliant flare of white light envelops the room. When it fades, we find ourselves back in Kate's holding cell. Kate stands beside the rough wooden bunk, looking around in confusion.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Convincing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Convincing)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Shutting
TEXT:

 "What I'm wondering is if she had a hand in the shutting down of the Beacon."

"I doubt it," Arraka says. "If their 'great leader' Heksol approved this project, he was the only one with the power to pull funding, and he didn't care about a batch of orphans. The Beacon was shut down before Meridith took over, so her actions probably had little influence."

"Arraka," Illuna interrupts, "shouldn't we be focusing on escaping this place instead of having casual conversations?"

"I AM focusing on escaping," Arraka says. "The only reason I'm continuing with the ritual is to keep my nerves steady while I map an escape plan."

