choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Convincing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Convincing)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Convincing
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch11_skip2 == 0) {
 "Ah!" Arraka says. "I think I get it now. What she needs to do to complete the ritual is to no longer intervene, and to let that caretaker rescue only the children that he got to save in the original timeline. That's going to take her a while to figure out, I guess... Alright, let's move on to the next scene."

We materialize in Diane's cell, where Kate is pleading once more with her friend, trying to prove she carries memories from the future and that they can escape the Beacon together.

"This isn't the first time I've been sent back..." Kate says urgently. "I can prove to you that I'm coming from the future. I know exactly when the caretakers will be bringing our meals, and what we will have for breakfast. I know when the caretaker who wants to help us escape will contact us, and I know what he will say. I know many things. And I also know that Olivia is an informant to the lessathi... We can't let her know about our escape plan, or she will tell them immediately. I'm going to try and convince the others myself, but you need to be the one to tell your brother about this. He won't trust anyone else..."

"If you're really serious about those things you said," Diane says, "then give me a list of everything you know, and I'll make sure to give it to Jason as soon as possible. I'm sure he would be much more inclined to take you seriously if he sees the proof with his own eyes."

"Don't worry," Kate says. "You'll have the list. I'm going to make sure that everything goes right this time. I won't fail again!"

"Oh, she will fail again!" Arraka sneers. "Have no doubt about that. Let's see if we can speed things up a bit, because there are going to be a lot of rescue attempts to go through."

She accelerates the flow, key events flashing past in rapid succession to sketch the wider pattern.

For the first few attempts, Kate focuses entirely on two things: winning over Jason, and rallying the other orphans without alerting Olivia.

Every time the loop resets, Kate provides precise predictions of coming events to Diane and Jason. Diane believes her quickly, but Jason remains obstinately skeptical, dismissing the details as surveillance tricks or remarkably lucky guesses.

}
#if(v_b2_ch11_skip2 == 1) {
 "Ah!" Arraka says. "I think I get it now. What she needs to do to complete the ritual is to no longer intervene, and to let that caretaker rescue only the children that he got to save in the original timeline. That's going to take her a while to figure out, I guess... Alright, let's see if we can make this a little quicker, then."

 She accelerates the flow, key events flashing past in rapid succession to sketch the wider pattern.

For the first few attempts, Kate focuses entirely on two things: winning over Jason, and rallying the other orphans without alerting Olivia.

Every time the loop resets, Kate provides precise predictions of coming events to Diane and Jason. Diane believes her quickly, but Jason remains obstinately skeptical, dismissing the details as surveillance tricks or remarkably lucky guesses.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
Convincing the other orphans proves even harder. Kate cannot risk explaining her time loops for fear someone will report her to the wardens. Lacking concrete proof that the caretaker is genuinely trustworthy, her insistence only deepens their paranoia. By the end of each attempt, someone panics and betrays the plan, or the day simply runs out and resets without progress.

After countless iterations, Kate finally finds a way to get everyone on her side and executes a flawless breakout beyond the perimeter. But the loop resets instantly. Saving the orphans is the exact opposite of what the ritual requires.

"Look at that..." I say, watching Kate struggle against the looping trap. "She must have spent weeks in this facility subjectively. How much time has passed in the real world?"

"Probably only a few minutes or so," Arraka says. "Time flows completely differently in each of these ritual worlds. The dwarf, the mute girl, and the revenant already finished their rituals. The healer is still busy with his, though."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
Convincing the other orphans proves even harder. Kate cannot risk explaining her time loops for fear someone will report her to the wardens. Lacking concrete proof that the caretaker is genuinely trustworthy, her insistence only deepens their paranoia. By the end of each attempt, someone panics and betrays the plan, or the day simply runs out and resets without progress.

After countless iterations, Kate finally finds a way to get everyone on her side and executes a flawless breakout beyond the perimeter. But the loop resets instantly. Saving the orphans is the exact opposite of what the ritual requires.

"Look at that..." I say, watching Kate struggle against the looping trap. "She must have spent weeks in this facility subjectively. How much time has passed in the real world?"

"Probably only a few minutes or so," Arraka says. "Time flows completely differently in each of these ritual worlds. The dwarf and the mute girl already finished their rituals. The healer and the herbalist are still busy with theirs, though."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Denial, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Denial) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Switching, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Switching) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Denial
TEXT:

 "So, are you any closer to finding a way that gets us out of here safely?" I say.

"It's hard to say," Arraka tells me. "I've been making progress, but there are still a lot of verifications to run. Like I said, I'm not making a move until I can be absolutely certain there are no risks."

"Right..." I say.

In the projection, Kate's attempts grow increasingly frantic with each new reset. She begins trying to save even Olivia from the Beacon, convinced that the time resets because someone was left behind. Several attempts end in her capture, though the day resets before the lessathi can commence conditioning or dissection.

Eventually, Kate orchestrates a complete escape that includes Olivia, every orphan crossing the boundary unharmed. Yet the loop resets just the same. The optimism on Kate's face shatters into hollow despair as the realization sets in that completing the ritual demands the exact opposite of what her conscience can bear.

"Hmm..." Arraka says. "I think she's finally starting to understand. I still expect her to remain in denial for at least five more time loops, though. Regardless, I've secured our extraction thread. Get ready to experience some severe nausea, because our comfort will be the very last thing on my mind while we escape."

A sharp headache spikes through my temples.

Reality snaps back in a blinding pulse.

We return to our physical bodies beneath the frozen trees of the Varathian forest, standing in the clearing where the trials began. Time remains locked still. Melindra, Leila, Hadrik, and the revenant stand nearby, watching us, while Daren and Kate stare blankly ahead, their eyes glowing with bright yellow light.

"We made it..." Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet, sounding both surprised and relieved. "We made it! We got out of there alive. Hahahahahaha! Take that, Magium, you son of a bitch!"

"Could you maybe avoid taunting the Magium when we are still technically standing right on its doorstep?" Illuna snaps.

"Relax!" Arraka says. "Everything is under control. I got us out of there alive, didn't I? And besides, I wasn't even insulting it. I was giving it a compliment, by treating it as if it were an actual person."

"You are acting as if you've already forgotten what can happen when you ignore my warnings..." Illuna says, her tone dropping to ice. "Would you perhaps like me to give you a small reminder by transporting us back into the world we just escaped from?"

Arraka falls completely silent. After several seconds of dead quiet, Illuna continues.

"You know that I can do it," she says. "You're the one who taught me everything I know, after all..."

"Alright, you win!" Arraka mutters, panic surfacing in her voice. "I'll shut my mouth, okay? It was just a little gloating, that's all... You don't have to get so upset over it!"

"Arraka sounding scared?" Hadrik says with a grin, though his eyes remain tense and guarded. "Now, that's something you don't see every day!"

choice(""If you think she's scared now, then you should have seen her earlier..."", B2-Ch11b-Scared, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Scared)
choice(""Trust me, if you'd have gone through what we just did, you'd be panicking like that too."", B2-Ch11b-Panicking, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Panicking)
choice(""Actually, you have a pretty uneasy look too. Did something happen during your ritual?"", B2-Ch11b-Uneasy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Uneasy)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Switching
TEXT:

 "Rose is still inside her ritual?" I say. "Can you show it to me? Or would that be too dangerous?"

"Moving between rituals is not a problem, because we remain linked to the Magium in our non-corporeal forms while we do it," Arraka says. "It's only when we're switching back to our regular bodies that we become vulnerable."

"So... can we go, then?" I say.

"Sure, I don't see why not..." Arraka says. "I was starting to get a little bored of this ritual, anyway."

The scenery dissolves, and we materialize in Thilias, in an empty alleyway within one of the wealthier districts. Nineteen-year-old Rose is on her knees in the dirt, tears streaming down her face.

"Please, stop making me do this..." Rose weeps into her hands. "I don't want to do this anymore. I'm sorry. I'm so sorry..."

"Wait, what's happening here?" I ask Arraka. "How did it come to this?"

"I'm trying to find out," Arraka says. "Just give me a minute, while I take a short look at what's been going on so far."

For twenty seconds, only the sound of Rose's ragged weeping breaks the quiet. Then Arraka speaks.

"Okay, so this ritual is a little different from the others," Arraka says. "Instead of forcing her to recreate only one event from her past, she actually needs to repeat more of them, in succession. Basically, she has to willingly ignore all of the slaves that have ever asked her for help, just like she's done in the past, except now she's also being forced to witness what happens with those slaves, after she refuses to help them. Some of them have been brutally murdered by their masters, some of them have been left to die of hunger, others have been tortured non-stop for weeks, until they went crazy. All sorts of stuff. I don't think this ritual of hers will end until she gets to see the fate of every single one of the slaves she's ever ignored during her lifetime."

choice(""That's terrible..."", B2-Ch11b-Terrible, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Terrible)
choice(""Seems fair."", B2-Ch11b-Fair, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Fair)
choice(""What about the cases where nothing really bad happened to the slaves afterwards?"", B2-Ch11b-Afterwards, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Afterwards)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Terrible
TEXT:

 "That's terrible..."

"You think so?" Arraka says. "Because as far as rituals go, this one seems pretty light, to me. I mean, take Flower's ritual, for example. She was given the illusion that she could save all of her friends from Olmnar, when it wasn't even her fault that she couldn't save them the first time. Now, that's real cruelty! This ritual, on the other hand, just forces Rose to see the direct consequences of her actions. It's not like she didn't know these things would happen, when she made her choices. She just chose to not really think about it, because it was more comfortable for her that way."

"Even so..." I say. "This ritual is still going a bit too far."

"If you say so," Arraka says.

Footsteps scrape along the cobblestones. An eighteen-year-old girl in dirty, worn-down clothes makes her way into the alleyway, limping heavily on her right leg. Her blonde hair is matted with grime, her tunic torn. A runaway slave, with no master in sight yet.

"No..." Rose scrambles to her feet and backs away, shock and horror twisting her face as she watches the girl approach. "No, no, no... Not this. Anything but this."

The slave girl hobbles closer, reaching out to grasp both of Rose's hands, her eyes wide with desperate pleading.

"Please, you have to help me..." the slave girl says. "My master is looking for me. He will come into this alleyway soon, and I can't outrun him. But if I hide in this abandoned shop, and you tell him that you saw me go the other way, maybe he'll believe you. I don't have anyone else to ask. You're my only hope. Please, don't abandon me..."

Rose shakes her head in agony, fresh tears spilling over her cheeks as she stares at the girl's hands. But time is running out.

Rose pulls her hands away, wipes her wet cheeks with her sleeve, and forces a warm smile onto her face. She gently takes the slave girl's hands and folds them together.

"You should not be doing this," Rose says, holding the girl fast, her voice trembling beneath the forced serenity. "As a slave, it is your duty to keep serving your master, no matter how harshly he may be treating you. Each one of us has their own purpose in life, and we cannot simply run away from it when things get too rough. Slaves are an important part of our society, and you should be proud of the contributions you are making towards the betterment of our civilization. Don't worry, I won't let go of your hands until your master comes here to get you. I'm sure that you will be thanking me later, after you've had the chance to calm down and think about everything more rationally."

With every word, Rose's voice grows shakier and her eyes well with tears, yet she holds that rigid, placid smile in place. The slave girl stares at her in disbelief, hoping against hope that it is only a cruel joke. When she realizes Rose is holding her fast, she thrashes wildly, trying to tear her wrists free.

"No!" the slave girl screams. "Let me go! I don't understand... How can you be so cruel? I've done nothing to you and yet... and yet..."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Commendable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Commendable)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Fair
TEXT:

 "Seems fair."

"I know, right?" Arraka says. "This ritual barely even compares to some of the others. I mean, take Illuna's ritual, for example. It's not like it was her fault that she needed to kill all those animals in the sacred forest. That all happened because of me. And yet, the ritual still forced her to repeat that massacre all over again, and to watch her friends die in front of her eyes. Now, that's real cruelty! This ritual, on the other hand, just forces Rose to see the direct consequences of her actions. It's not like she didn't know these things would happen, when she made her choices. She just chose to not really think about it, because it was more comfortable for her that way."

Illuna's right hand closes into a tight fist at the mention of the massacre, but she remains silent.

Footsteps scrape along the cobblestones. An eighteen-year-old girl in dirty, worn-down clothes makes her way into the alleyway, limping heavily on her right leg. Her blonde hair is matted with grime, her tunic torn. A runaway slave, with no master in sight yet.

"No..." Rose scrambles to her feet and backs away, shock and horror twisting her face as she watches the girl approach. "No, no, no... Not this. Anything but this."

The slave girl hobbles closer, reaching out to grasp both of Rose's hands, her eyes wide with desperate pleading.

"Please, you have to help me..." the slave girl says. "My master is looking for me. He will come into this alleyway soon, and I can't outrun him. But if I hide in this abandoned shop, and you tell him that you saw me go the other way, maybe he'll believe you. I don't have anyone else to ask. You're my only hope. Please, don't abandon me..."

Rose shakes her head in agony, fresh tears spilling over her cheeks as she stares at the girl's hands. But time is running out.

Rose pulls her hands away, wipes her wet cheeks with her sleeve, and forces a warm smile onto her face. She gently takes the slave girl's hands and folds them together.

"You should not be doing this," Rose says, holding the girl fast, her voice trembling beneath the forced serenity. "As a slave, it is your duty to keep serving your master, no matter how harshly he may be treating you. Each one of us has their own purpose in life, and we cannot simply run away from it when things get too rough. Slaves are an important part of our society, and you should be proud of the contributions you are making towards the betterment of our civilization. Don't worry, I won't let go of your hands until your master comes here to get you. I'm sure that you will be thanking me later, after you've had the chance to calm down and think about everything more rationally."

With every word, Rose's voice grows shakier and her eyes well with tears, yet she holds that rigid, placid smile in place. The slave girl stares at her in disbelief, hoping against hope that it is only a cruel joke. When she realizes Rose is holding her fast, she thrashes wildly, trying to tear her wrists free.

"No!" the slave girl screams. "Let me go! I don't understand... How can you be so cruel? I've done nothing to you and yet... and yet..."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Commendable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Commendable)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Afterwards
TEXT:

 "What about the cases where nothing really bad happened to the slaves afterwards?"

"I'm pretty sure that those are just being skipped," Arraka says. "It wouldn't make for much of a show if they didn't cut out the boring parts, now would it?"

"But what if she forgets what she said to these slaves?" I say. "Would the ritual just keep going on forever?"

"Nah," Arraka says. "This ritual has access to her memories, so it knows exactly what she remembers and what she doesn't. If it's something deeply buried in her subconscious that she doesn't want to remember, then she will probably be forced to remember it, but if it's just a matter of no longer knowing the exact words she used, it probably won't make that much of a difference. After all, this ritual's purpose is to maximize her amount of suffering, not to annoy the hell out of her."

"I suppose that makes sense," I say.

Footsteps scrape along the cobblestones. An eighteen-year-old girl in dirty, worn-down clothes makes her way into the alleyway, limping heavily on her right leg. Her blonde hair is matted with grime, her tunic torn. A runaway slave, with no master in sight yet.

"No..." Rose scrambles to her feet and backs away, shock and horror twisting her face as she watches the girl approach. "No, no, no... Not this. Anything but this."

The slave girl hobbles closer, reaching out to grasp both of Rose's hands, her eyes wide with desperate pleading.

"Please, you have to help me..." the slave girl says. "My master is looking for me. He will come into this alleyway soon, and I can't outrun him. But if I hide in this abandoned shop, and you tell him that you saw me go the other way, maybe he'll believe you. I don't have anyone else to ask. You're my only hope. Please, don't abandon me..."

Rose shakes her head in agony, fresh tears spilling over her cheeks as she stares at the girl's hands. But time is running out.

Rose pulls her hands away, wipes her wet cheeks with her sleeve, and forces a warm smile onto her face. She gently takes the slave girl's hands and folds them together.

"You should not be doing this," Rose says, holding the girl fast, her voice trembling beneath the forced serenity. "As a slave, it is your duty to keep serving your master, no matter how harshly he may be treating you. Each one of us has their own purpose in life, and we cannot simply run away from it when things get too rough. Slaves are an important part of our society, and you should be proud of the contributions you are making towards the betterment of our civilization. Don't worry, I won't let go of your hands until your master comes here to get you. I'm sure that you will be thanking me later, after you've had the chance to calm down and think about everything more rationally."

With every word, Rose's voice grows shakier and her eyes well with tears, yet she holds that rigid, placid smile in place. The slave girl stares at her in disbelief, hoping against hope that it is only a cruel joke. When she realizes Rose is holding her fast, she thrashes wildly, trying to tear her wrists free.

"No!" the slave girl screams. "Let me go! I don't understand... How can you be so cruel? I've done nothing to you and yet... and yet..."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Commendable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Commendable)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Commendable
TEXT:

 "There she is!" a man in velvet robes shouts as he strides into the alleyway. "I found her. Get over here and help me capture her!"

Two armed retainers rush in behind him, converging on Rose and the slave girl.

"Let go of my hands!" the slave girl cries, thrashing with desperate strength. "I beg you! I can't go back there... He's going to kill me. You have to understand. You have to!"

The moment the noble reaches them, Rose releases her grip. The man seizes the slave by the arm and pulls her toward him with enough violence to wrench her shoulder.

"I hope you've had your fun," the noble tells the slave, his eyes cold and furious, "because after what you've done today, I will be expecting you to accept your punishment without even the slightest resistance. Do you understand?"

"Yes, sir..." the slave girl whispers, her whole body trembling in fear.

"Good," the noble says.

He turns to Rose.

"Thank you very much for your help!" the noble says. "Who knows where this slave might have run off to, if you hadn't held on to her?"

"Oh, there's no need to thank me," Rose says, that placid smile holding on even as fresh tears spill over her eyelashes. "We should all be trying our best to help our fellow citizens in their time of need!"

"I'm sorry, this must have been a very terrifying experience for you," the noble says, noting the tears. "Here, allow me to reward you for your efforts."

"No!" Rose pulls back as he produces a pouch full of gold coins from his pocket. "I'm not looking for any reward. I only did what I thought was right..."

"How commendable!" the noble says, genuine respect in his voice as he pockets the purse. "It is rare to see someone with such high moral values in this day and age. Whatever you do, remember to never let go of those principles, for it is these principles that truly make us human!"

"Yes, sir, I will remember..." Rose whispers.

"Very well! In that case, we will take our leave, and bother you no longer. Farewell, and thank you once again!"

"Goodbye..." Rose says, watching in silence as the nobleman and his retainers drag the girl out of the alley.

"Ahahahahahaha!" Arraka's laughter rings out. "Did you hear that? Did you hear what she said to that slave? Oh man, I can't believe she actually used to think like that, six years ago. How brainwashed can you be? And then there was that noble with his speech about principles! I can't take it anymore. Ahahahaha!"

A brilliant flash of white light tears through the scene.

We materialize inside an opulent manor. The nobleman from before is beating his slave with a heavy cane. The slave girl lies motionless on the marble floor, no longer making a sound beneath the blows, while Rose stands right in front of her in spectral form, forced to watch every strike in silent, unblinking horror.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Reunion, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Reunion)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Reunion
TEXT:

 "Right, I think I might finally be getting somewhere with our escape plan," Arraka says. "Just give me a few seconds and I'll--"

"Barry? Illuna?..."

Rose turns toward us, her spectral eyes locking directly onto ours.

"You can see us?" I ask, taken aback.

"She is spending half of her ritual in her semi-transparent form," Arraka mutters. "She's practically in the same world as us, now. Aaaanyway, I'm sorry to interrupt your reunion, but our way out is finally clear, and I don't plan to waste this opportunity. You'd better get yourselves ready for some strong nausea, because our comfort will be the very last thing on my mind while we escape this place."

A sharp headache spikes through my temples.

Reality snaps back in a blinding pulse.

We return to our regular, non-transparent bodies beneath the frozen trees of the Varathian forest, standing in the clearing where our rituals started, next to all of our friends. Time remains locked still. Melindra, Leila and Hadrik watch us, while Daren, Kate and Rose stare blankly in front of them, with bright, glowy eyes.

"We made it..." Arraka says, sounding both surprised and relieved at the same time. "We made it! We got out of there alive. Hahahahahaha! Take that, Magium, you son of a bitch!"

"Could you maybe avoid taunting the Magium when we are still technically standing right on its doorstep?" Illuna snaps.

"Relax!" Arraka says. "Everything is under control. I got us out of there alive, didn't I? And besides, I wasn't even insulting it. I was giving it a compliment, by treating it as if it were an actual person."

"You are acting as if you've already forgotten what can happen when you ignore my warnings..." Illuna says, in a serious tone. "Would you perhaps like me to give you a small reminder by transporting us back into the world we just escaped from?"

Arraka falls completely silent. After several seconds of dead quiet, Illuna continues.

"You know that I can do it," she says. "You're the one who taught me everything I know, after all..."

"Alright, you win!" Arraka says, in a somewhat panicked tone. "I'll shut my mouth, okay? It was just a little gloating, that's all... You don't have to get so upset over it!"

"Arraka sounding scared?" Hadrik says, with a grin, although I can see from the uneasy look in his eyes that he is still affected by whatever happened to him in his ritual. "Now, that's something you don't see every day!"

choice(""If you think she's scared now, then you should have seen her earlier..."", B2-Ch11b-Scared, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Scared)
choice(""Trust me, if you'd have gone through what we just did, you'd be panicking like that too."", B2-Ch11b-Panicking, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Panicking)
choice(""Actually, you have a pretty uneasy look too. Did something happen during your ritual?"", B2-Ch11b-Uneasy, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Uneasy)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Scared
TEXT:

 "If you think she's scared now, then you should have seen her earlier..."

"Well, I'll be damned..." Hadrik says. "Now I'm starting to regret that I wasn't there with you guys in your ritual. Mine was boring, in comparison."

"Actually it wasn't our ritual, it was--" I start to say, but Arraka drowns out my voice.

"Oh, your ritual was boring, you say!" Arraka says loudly. "Are you sure you're not trying to hide something? Because the way I remember your ritual, it was anything but boring..."

"What do you know about my ritual?..." Hadrik says, as he regains the uneasiness from before in his eyes.

"More than you think, dwarf," Arraka says. "And I'm not talking only about your trial from the God of Time. I've looked through some of your memories too, since they were pretty much out in the open, when you were trapped in there. I saw some very interesting things, while I was looking through the memories that were related to your ritual. Want me to tell you what I found out?"

"You're bluffing," Hadrik says, as I see him starting to sweat a little. "You're just trying to bait me into giving the information away myself. How stupid do you think I am?"

"Well, I don't know, dwarf..." Arraka says. "How stupid are you?"

For the next few seconds, the two of them remain completely silent, as Hadrik watches Arraka's amulet intensely, while a few more drops of sweat form on his forehead.

"But seriously, though, I was only joking," Arraka cackles suddenly. "I have absolutely no idea what was going on in your ritual. The only reason why I said all that was because you pissed me off, and I wanted to make you sweat like a pig. Who's the one panicking now, eh? Aha- Ahahahahahaha!"

"Blasted old hag..." Hadrik says, as he relaxes a little.

"So, you really didn't get to see Hadrik's ritual, then?" I ask Arraka.

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
"Nah," Arraka says. "There were just too many of them for me to have time to see them all. Well, anyway, it's pretty obvious that his ritual wasn't all sunshine and rainbows either, but I guess we're just going to have to wait until he feels like telling us about it, if we want to find out what it was. Personally, I don't mind all that much. It's not like we don't have anything better to do at this point than to listen to his sob story, you know what I'm saying?"

Not long after we finish our conversation, Daren's eyes lose their bright yellow color, as he finally exits his trance as well. From the look on his face, I can easily tell that he's pretty upset.

"Oh, look, the healer is out!" Arraka says. "How's it going, Daren? Had fun in your ritual?"

Daren chooses to completely ignore Arraka's question, and he turns to me, instead.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
"Nah," Arraka says. "There were just too many of them for me to have time to see them all. Well, anyway, it's pretty obvious that his ritual wasn't all sunshine and rainbows either, but I guess we're just going to have to wait until he feels like telling us about it, if we want to find out what it was. Personally, I don't mind all that much. It's not like we don't have anything better to do at this point than to listen to his sob story, you know what I'm saying?"

Not long after we finish our conversation, Daren's eyes lose their bright yellow color, as he finally exits his trance as well. The look that he has now on his face is darker than it's ever been before. And I'm not surprised. From his perspective, it was probably only a few moments ago that he had to willingly let the slave girl die in front of his eyes, so he could complete his trial.

"Oh, look, the healer is out!" Arraka says. "How's it going, Daren? Had fun in your ritual?"

Daren chooses to completely ignore Arraka's question, and he turns to me, instead.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Fatigue, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Fatigue)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Panicking
TEXT:

 "Trust me, if you'd have gone through what we just did, you'd be panicking like that too."

"Well, you know me, Barry," Hadrik says. "Even if I'm scared out of my wits, I still could never pass up a good challenge!"

"Is that so?" Arraka says. "In that case you must have absolutely loved your ritual! Because from what I saw, to say that your trial was a challenge would be quite an understatement."

"What do you know about my ritual?..." Hadrik says, as he regains the uneasiness from before in his eyes.

