choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Fatigue, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Fatigue)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Fatigue
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "How many are still inside their rituals?" Daren asks me. "Only Kate and Rose?"

"Yeah, only Kate and Rose," I say. "You can tell from the glowy eyes. I think that we need to wait for everyone to be done with the first stage, before we can pass on to the next one."

"I can't say that I'm looking forward to it," Daren says. "Where is the God of Time? Is he still watching us?"

"No, he went back to the magical plane," I say. "He'll come back again at the end of the ritual, according to Arraka."

While Daren and I are talking, the yellow light fades from Kate's eyes as she pulls out of her trance. The grief and exhaustion on her face are wrenching to watch—not just sorrow and regret, but the hollow resignation in her eyes that shows just how deep the trial cut into her. Realizing she is no longer alone, Kate instantly pulls up a mask of dull, extreme fatigue.

"Is everyone else already done with their ritual?" Kate asks, her voice flat. "And where is the God of Time? I thought he was supposed to oversee these trials until their very end."

"We're all done with our rituals, except for Rose," Daren says. "The God of Time apparently went back to the magical plane, and he'll only come back at the end."

"If that's true, then maybe we can make some plan against him," Kate says, looking between us. "Maybe we can find a way to sabotage his ritual while he's not here to keep an eye on us..."

"You can't sabotage the rituals..." Melindra says from the edge of the clearing. "Eiden has tried and failed in the past. If he couldn't do it, then how could we?"

"Eiden isn't all-knowing," Daren says. "Just because he failed, it doesn't mean that we won't succeed."

"Oh?" Melindra crosses her arms. "Then how do you propose to start this 'sabotage'?"

"We'll... figure something out..." Daren mutters.

"If you say so," Melindra says.

The light fades from Rose's eyes as she finishes her ritual as well. She stands rigid, staring blankly ahead without taking notice of us, her face pale and completely horrified.

"Rose..." Daren says, stepping toward her with deep concern. "Are you alright?"

Rose gives no sign of having heard him, and she keeps staring in front of her, without saying a word.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "How many are still inside their rituals?" Daren asks me. "Only Kate?"

"Yeah, only Kate," I say. "You can tell from the glowy eyes. I think that we need to wait for everyone to be done with the first stage, before we can pass on to the next one."

"I can't say that I'm looking forward to it," Daren says. "Where is the God of Time? Is he still watching us?"

"No, he went back to the magical plane," I say. "He'll come back again at the end of the ritual, according to Arraka."

While Daren and I are talking, the yellow light fades from Kate's eyes as she pulls out of her trance. The grief and exhaustion on her face are wrenching to watch—not just sorrow and regret, but the hollow resignation in her eyes that shows just how deep the trial cut into her. Realizing she is no longer alone, Kate instantly pulls up a mask of dull, extreme fatigue.

"Is everyone else already done with their ritual?" Kate asks, her voice flat. "And where is the God of Time? I thought he was supposed to oversee these trials until their very end."

"The first stage of the ritual is done, and now we're waiting for the second," Daren says. "The God of Time apparently went back to the magical plane, and he'll only come back at the end."

"If that's true, then maybe we can make some plan against him," Kate says, looking between us. "Maybe we can find a way to sabotage his ritual while he's not here to keep an eye on us..."

"You can't sabotage the rituals..." Melindra says from the edge of the clearing. "Eiden has tried and failed in the past. If he couldn't do it, then how could we?"

"Eiden isn't all-knowing," Daren says. "Just because he failed, it doesn't mean that we won't succeed."

"Oh?" Melindra crosses her arms. "Then how do you propose to start this 'sabotage'?"

"We'll... figure something out..." Daren mutters.

"If you say so," Melindra says.

"If you're looking to sabotage the ritual, then I might be able to offer some assistance," Rose's voice cuts in quietly.

We'd all been ignoring the revenant until this point, but now every head snaps toward her at the perimeter.

"And why would we want any help from you, revenant?" Daren says, disgust hardening his voice.

"Because this ritual is as much a problem for me as it is for you," the revenant says calmly. "...And also because as much as I've tried to avoid it, there are traces of your friend Rose within me that will always be a part of who I am. At the very least, I know that Rose would have wanted us to work together, despite our differences, since nothing would have been more important to her in this situation than to make sure that you are safe."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Herbalist, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Herbalist) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
